UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyC$# Selling in Germany as a non-EU seller: importer and authorised representative duties

**Short answer:** Non-EU manufacturers cannot simply ship into Germany; EU law assigns compliance duties to supply-chain economic operators. Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 requires an EU-established economic operator for listed harmonisation legislation, to hold the declaration of conformity and cooperate with market surveillance authorities. Importers must verify conformity before sale; authorised representatives act under a written mandate.

## Key facts

- Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 requires an economic operator established in the EU for products subject to the harmonisation legislation listed in its annexes.
- The economic operator's tasks include holding the EU declaration of conformity, cooperating with market surveillance authorities and handling corrective actions.
- Importers must verify, before placing products on the market, that the manufacturer carried out the conformity assessment, that technical documentation exists, and that products bear the required markings and traceability information.
- A manufacturer may appoint an EU-based authorised representative by written mandate; the mandate's scope determines what the representative may and must do.
- Where no importer or authorised representative exists, fulfilment service providers storing and shipping products in the EU can fall into the economic-operator role for the products they handle.
- German authorities enforce at borders and on online marketplaces and can order withdrawals or recalls.
- Contact details of the responsible economic operator must appear on the product or packaging as the legislation requires.

## The economic-operator requirement

Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 requires an economic operator established in the EU for products subject to listed harmonisation legislation. That operator handles tasks such as holding the declaration of conformity and cooperating with market surveillance authorities. This is the single most important structural fact for non-EU sellers: there must be a reachable, responsible entity inside the EU for the product, and "the factory in Shenzhen" does not satisfy it.

The requirement applies to products covered by the EU harmonisation legislation listed in the regulation's annexes, which includes most CE-marked product categories. The economic operator can be the manufacturer (if established in the EU), the importer, an authorised representative, or a fulfilment service provider. The regulation defines the tasks: keeping the declaration of conformity available, cooperating with authorities including providing documents in a language they understand, and informing authorities where there is reason to believe a product presents a risk.

For a non-EU brand, the practical question is who in the chain will play this role for each product line. The answer must be settled before the first unit ships, because marketplaces, customs and market surveillance authorities all ask for it, and the product or its packaging must carry the operator's contact details. A common failure mode is assuming the role is covered when it is not: for example, a brand that ships DDP to EU consumers with no EU importer and no appointed representative may discover that its fulfilment warehouse has become the economic operator by default, without any agreement on who holds the technical file.

## Importer verification duties

Importers must verify that the manufacturer carried out the conformity assessment, that the technical documentation exists, and that products bear the required markings and traceability information. Document each verification; "the supplier said so" is not evidence. The importer is the compliance gatekeeper for products entering the EU market, and the duties are active, not ceremonial.

A proper pre-market verification program covers: confirming the product bears the CE marking (where required) affixed according to the graphic rules; checking that the EU Declaration of Conformity exists, identifies the product and lists the correct directives and standards; confirming the technical documentation exists and can be produced; verifying traceability labelling (product identification, manufacturer name and address, and the importer's own name and address on the product, packaging or accompanying documents); and checking that instructions and safety information are in the required languages. Where risk warrants it, the importer should sample-test products, particularly for new suppliers, new product categories or categories with known non-compliance rates.

Verification must be documented per product and per shipment where appropriate. A verification file for each SKU, holding the declaration copy, the labelling artwork check, the language check and any test reports, is what an authority expects to see. Importers must also monitor the market: keep a register of complaints and non-conforming products where appropriate, and act immediately on non-compliance by withdrawing or recalling products and informing the authorities in the member states where the product was sold.

## Authorised representatives: the written mandate

A manufacturer may appoint an EU-based authorised representative by written mandate to perform specified tasks. The mandate's scope determines what the representative may and must do; keep the signed mandate on file. The authorised representative is the cleanest structure for a non-EU brand selling directly to EU consumers: the brand appoints a professional representative in the EU, the mandate defines the duties (holding the declaration, cooperating with authorities, handling corrective actions), and the representative's contact details go on the product or packaging.

Draft the mandate precisely. It should name the products or product families covered, list the tasks delegated (referencing the regulation's articles), set the representative's authority to act with market surveillance authorities, define information flows (the manufacturer must give the representative access to the declaration and technical documentation), and allocate costs and liabilities, including who pays for testing, who funds a recall and how change notifications work. A vague mandate creates exactly the ambiguity the regulation is trying to eliminate.

Choosing a representative is a due-diligence exercise. Verify that the provider is established in the EU, understands the product category's legislation, has a process for holding and producing documentation on demand, and carries appropriate professional coverage. The cheapest mandate on the market is not a bargain if the provider cannot produce your technical file within the authority's deadline.

## Fulfilment service providers: the default operator

Where no importer or authorised representative exists, fulfilment service providers storing and shipping products in the EU can fall into the economic-operator role for the products they handle. Clarify responsibilities contractually. This provision exists to close the enforcement gap for direct-to-consumer imports: a non-EU seller holding stock in an EU warehouse and shipping to EU consumers, with no EU importer and no appointed representative, leaves the fulfilment provider as the operator of last resort.

For brands, the lesson is to never drift into this position accidentally. If your fulfilment provider becomes the economic operator by default, the provider may not hold your technical documentation, may not understand your product's legislation, and will look to you contractually for the costs of any enforcement action. Either appoint an authorised representative or structure a genuine importer relationship; do not rely on the warehouse.

For fulfilment providers and 3PLs, the lesson is the reverse: understand that handling certain products can attach economic-operator duties, and address it in client contracts with clear allocation of documentation duties, information flows and costs. Both sides should confirm the arrangement in writing before the first pallet arrives.

## Market surveillance in practice

German authorities enforce at borders and on online marketplaces, and can order withdrawals or recalls. Keep contact details of the responsible economic operator on the product or packaging as the legislation requires. Enforcement in Germany is active and multi-channel: customs can hold consignments at the border pending documentation checks, market surveillance authorities test products purchased from online marketplaces, and marketplaces themselves increasingly require operator and registration details as a condition of listing.

When an authority acts, speed and documentation decide the outcome. A complete technical file, a valid declaration, correct labelling and a cooperative economic operator typically resolve a documentation check quickly. Missing documents, an unreachable operator or incorrect markings escalate to withdrawal orders, recalls and publication in Safety Gate, the EU rapid-alert system. The cost difference between the two outcomes is orders of magnitude, which is why the verification and documentation duties in the earlier sections are commercial necessities, not bureaucratic overhead.

Online sellers face an additional enforcement surface: the listing itself. Under the GPSR's distance-sale rules, listings targeting EU consumers must carry manufacturer and responsible-person details, product identification and safety information before the consumer buys. A compliant product behind a non-compliant listing is still an enforcement target.

## Choosing your operator structure

| Structure | How it works | Best for | Watch out for |
|---|---|---|---|
| EU importer | An EU-established company imports and places the goods on the market | Brands selling through EU distribution | The importer needs real verification capacity, not just a name on the invoice |
| Authorised representative | Non-EU manufacturer appoints an EU representative by written mandate | Direct-to-consumer brands with no EU importer | The mandate must precisely define tasks, products and cost allocation |
| Fulfilment service provider | The EU warehouse operator falls into the role by default | Nobody by design | Accidental coverage: no documentation chain, no agreed responsibilities |
| EU-established manufacturer | The brand's own EU entity acts as manufacturer | Brands with EU operations | The entity must genuinely perform the manufacturer's duties |

Most non-EU sellers end up with either a real importer or an authorised representative. The fulfilment-provider default is a backstop, not a strategy.

## Compliance checklist

- [ ] Determine whether your products fall under the harmonisation legislation listed in Regulation (EU) 2019/1020.
- [ ] Designate the EU-based economic operator for each product line before the first shipment; never leave the role to default.
- [ ] If using an authorised representative, execute a precise written mandate and keep the signed original on file.
- [ ] Build the importer verification file per SKU: declaration copy, labelling and language checks, test reports where risk warrants.
- [ ] Put the responsible operator's contact details on the product or packaging as the legislation requires.
- [ ] Ensure the operator holds (or can immediately obtain) the EU declaration of conformity and the technical documentation.
- [ ] Clarify fulfilment-provider responsibilities contractually; confirm who holds what if the provider could fall into the operator role.
- [ ] Set up complaints monitoring, withdrawal/recall procedures and authority-notification workflows.
- [ ] Audit online listings for the required manufacturer, responsible-person, product and safety information.

## What this means for businesses

For businesses, the economic-operator requirement is the structural decision that underpins all other EU compliance: without a designated, documented, reachable operator in the EU, the product cannot lawfully be placed on the market, however good the technical file is. The practical program is therefore contractual and documentary as much as technical: mandates, verification files, labelling with operator details, and a fulfilment contract that cannot accidentally make the warehouse your compliance department. Budget for a professional authorised representative as a fixed market-entry cost if you sell direct to consumers, and treat the operator designation as a living record reviewed whenever the supply chain, the product range or the fulfilment setup changes. However, the operator does not replace the manufacturer's own duties; it adds the EU contact point and verification layer the enforcement system requires.

**Frequently asked questions:**

## Can I sell into Germany with no EU presence at all?

Not for products under the listed harmonisation legislation. Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 requires an economic operator established in the EU. Non-EU brands typically use an EU importer, an authorised representative appointed by written mandate, or in some structures a fulfilment service provider.

## What is the difference between an importer and an authorised representative?

An importer buys or takes title to the goods and places them on the EU market in its own right, carrying the full importer verification and post-market duties. An authorised representative is appointed by the (non-EU) manufacturer under a written mandate to perform specified tasks, such as holding the declaration and cooperating with authorities, without taking title to the goods.

## Does my fulfilment warehouse count as my economic operator?

It can, by default, where no importer or authorised representative exists for products the provider stores and ships in the EU. That is a backstop, not a strategy: clarify responsibilities contractually and designate a proper operator instead of drifting into default coverage.

## What must appear on the product or packaging?

The legislation requires the responsible economic operator's contact details (name, registered trade name or mark, and address) on the product or, where the product's nature does not allow it, on the packaging or accompanying documentation, alongside manufacturer and importer traceability information.

## What happens during a market-surveillance check?

Authorities typically ask the economic operator for the EU declaration of conformity and the technical documentation, and check markings, labelling and listing information. Complete documentation and a cooperative operator resolve most checks; missing documents or an unreachable operator escalate to withdrawals, recalls and Safety Gate notifications.

## Is a verbal agreement with my supplier enough for verification?

No. Document each verification; "the supplier said so" is not evidence. Keep a verification file per SKU with the declaration copy, labelling and language checks, and test reports where risk warrants.

## Assumptions and limitations

- The regulation lists the covered legislation in its annexes; confirm your product's legislation is in scope in the consolidated text.
- This guide addresses the standard structures; sector legislation can add operator-specific duties.
- National enforcement practice varies; Germany's is described here as active at borders and marketplaces, which reflects the enforcement design rather than a statistical claim.

## Official sources

- [Regulation (EU) 2019/1020 on EUR-Lex](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32019R1020)
- [Regulation (EU) 2023/988 (GPSR) on EUR-Lex (English)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2023/988/oj/eng)

Last verified: 2026-09-29.

